{"answer":"Large language models can actively gather information for better product recommendations by generating additional user- and item-level context from existing metadata or interaction sequences. In LaMAR, an LLM few-shotly infers latent semantic aspects of a user’s intent and relationships between items, producing auxiliary signals (e.g., inferred usage scenarios, item intents, thematic summaries) that enrich the training data for sequential recommenders [1]. For cold-start items, LLMs can also synthesize augmented interactions and use reinforcement learning to choose which user histories to pair with cold-start items, optimizing downstream cold-start recall [10].

They can also gather information through interactive preference elicitation in multi-turn systems. Dynamic, stage-aware conversational strategies explicitly ask users for informative preference cues, where attribute-based inquiries work early and item-based strategies become better as preferences refine [5]. Another approach selects which conversational interactions to use by maximizing expected reduction in the assistant’s uncertainty over recommendations, turning that “informativeness” into a training reward [8].

In addition, LLMs can gather (and update) information about the available catalog itself by using a self-refreshing retrieval layer that ingests live feeds and synchronizes changes into an index, while the LLM focuses on intent classification and preference elicitation [7]. These approaches differ in whether they gather information from user behavior (e.g., profiles, elicited preferences, synthesized interactions) [1,5,8,9,10] or from continuously changing product data [7].","grounded":true}
